#### 8. Exception Handling in Tests
**Ref:** Peruma et al. (2019); testsmells.org  
**Affected:** `ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsValidationError` (line 105)

```csharp
try
{
    processor.ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.Fail("Expected an exception but none was thrown");
}
catch (ValidationException ex)
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Order must contain at least one item", ex.Message);
}
```

**Risk:** Manual try/catch duplicates logic the framework provides. If a *different* exception type is thrown, the test reports an unhelpful framework error rather than a clear mismatch. The pattern is error-prone (forgetting `Assert.Fail` means a silent pass).

**Fix:** Use `Assert.ThrowsException<T>`:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsValidationError()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order();

    var ex = Assert.ThrowsException<ValidationException>(
        () => processor.ProcessOrder(order));

    Assert.AreEqual("Order must contain at least one item", ex.Message);
}
```

